# Project Development Guidelines## Testing Policy
**DO NOT** create, run, or suggest tests unless explicitly requested by the user
**DO NOT** add test files, test cases, or testing frameworks without explicit instruction
Focus on implementation and functionality over test coverage
## Documentation Policy
**DO NOT** create new documentation files or markdown files unless explicitly requested
**ALWAYS** update existing documentation when making changes
Refer to and update standard documents rather than creating new ones
Keep documentation changes minimal and focused
## Script and File Management
**ALWAYS** update existing standard scripts rather than creating new ones
**DO NOT** create temporary fixes or temporary scripts
If manual steps are required for a temporary fix, clearly call out these manual steps to the user
Use existing build scripts, deployment scripts, and configuration files
Maintain consistency with existing project structure
## Credit Optimization
**ALWAYS** take the most efficient path that uses fewer credits
Prefer targeted file updates over reading entire codebases
Use specific file operations rather than broad searches when possible
Minimize redundant operations and tool calls
Focus on the minimal viable solution that addresses the requirement
## Code Changes
Make precise, targeted changes to existing files
Maintain existing code patterns and conventions
Update related files when making changes (e.g., update types when changing interfaces)
Preserve existing functionality while adding new features
## Database Changes
**ALWAYS** record every DB schema change as a new numbered, idempotent .sql migration in backend/src/database/migrations/ (e.g. 014_*.sql)
**DO NOT** alter the schema ad-hoc or edit already-applied migration files; add a new migration instead
Use idempotent statements (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN) so migrations can run safely more than once
Keep 000_full_schema.sql (the consolidated fresh-install schema) in sync when adding new tables/columns
Apply new migrations to production before deploying code that depends on them, and clearly call out the migration step to the user
## Production Readiness
**ALWAYS** remove excessive console.log statements and debug logs before production deployment
**ALWAYS** verify database migrations are applied to production before deploying code changes
**ALWAYS** check for and remove development-only debugging code
**ALWAYS** perform a complete rebuild of both frontend and backend before production deployment
**ALWAYS** run complete testing (TypeScript compilation, linting, build verification) before production deployment
Ensure error handling is production-appropriate (no sensitive information exposed)
Verify all environment variables are properly configured for production